duckdb for Debian / Ubuntu
==========================

This is a packaging fork of https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb that
adds the debian/ directory needed to produce .deb files.  It is
unofficial; the upstream DuckDB project does not currently ship
Debian packages.

  - End users wanting to install: see debian/README.distribution
    (GitHub release downloads now, OBS apt repository planned).
  - Maintainers publishing to OBS: see debian/README.OBS.

The remainder of this file documents the source-package layout and
the branch-rebase workflow.

Branch layout (DEP-14)
----------------------

  upstream/latest   mirrors the upstream main branch verbatim, no
                    Debian-specific files.
  debian/latest     upstream/latest plus the debian/ directory in
                    this tree.  This is the branch from which
                    packages are built.

To take new upstream commits:

  git fetch upstream
  git checkout upstream/latest
  git merge --ff-only upstream/main
  git checkout debian/latest
  git rebase upstream/latest

The packaging is almost entirely additive: it adds the debian/
directory but does not modify upstream source files directly.  The
single quilt patch in debian/patches/ is the sole exception, and it
touches src/CMakeLists.txt only; merge conflicts on rebase are
therefore rare and limited to that one file.

Version numbering
-----------------

Package versions are of the form

  <upstream-x.y.z>+git<YYYYMMDD>.<short-hash>-<debian-revision>

for development snapshots, or

  <upstream-x.y.z>-<debian-revision>

for builds of a tagged upstream release.  The Debian revision is
incremented for packaging-only changes between upstream syncs.

Binary packages
---------------

  duckdb              command-line shell
  libduckdb1          shared library (SONAME = MAJOR only, see
                      debian/patches/soversion-major-only.patch)
  libduckdb-dev       headers, .so symlink, CMake config, demo_capi
                      example sources

The upstream CMake also builds libduckdb_static.a.  We do not ship
it.  Users who need a fully static DuckDB should use a musl-based
distribution.  The archive is discarded in debian/rules.

Compiler
--------

Build-Depends specifies g++ (>= 11), which is the gcc that ships in
all currently-supported Debian and Ubuntu LTS targets (Debian
trixie/sid, Ubuntu jammy/noble).  This is a packaging choice for
predictable C++17 + hardening defaults — upstream's own minimum is
just C++11.  To build with clang instead, set CC and CXX before
dpkg-buildpackage:

  CC=clang CXX=clang++ dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

No clang package is listed in Build-Depends so that the default OBS
chroot stays minimal; users who need clang must arrange it
themselves at build time.

Quilt patches
-------------

  soversion-major-only.patch
      Sets the SONAME to MAJOR only (libduckdb.so.1) instead of
      MAJOR.MINOR.  Activated by passing -DSET_DUCKDB_LIBRARY_VERSION=ON
      from debian/rules, since upstream's CMake leaves the version
      block guarded by an OFF-by-default option.  See the patch
      header for the rationale.

Third-party licensing
---------------------

DuckDB vendors a number of upstream libraries under third_party/.
debian/copyright delegates to the per-component LICENSE files
inside third_party/<name>/LICENSE rather than reproducing them
inline; libduckdb-dev additionally ships those LICENSE files under
/usr/share/doc/libduckdb-dev/third_party-licenses/.  See the
"Files: third_party/*" stanza in debian/copyright for the
components that do not currently carry an in-tree LICENSE file
(catch, imdb, thrift).

Extensions
----------

The following in-tree extensions are statically linked into the
shared library and the CLI; see debian/rules:

  autocomplete       SQL tab-completion in the REPL
  core_functions     standard built-in functions (string, math, date)
  icu                Unicode collation + IANA timezones
  json               read_json() and JSON manipulation
  parquet            read_parquet() and Parquet writers

Deliberately excluded:

  delta              experimental, requires a Rust toolchain
                     (delta-kernel-rs).  Re-enable by adding rustc
                     and cargo to Build-Depends and "delta" to
                     DUCKDB_EXTENSIONS in debian/rules.
  jemalloc           statically vendors its own jemalloc, which
                     conflicts with Debian's libjemalloc2 debug
                     tooling and surprises operators expecting the
                     system allocator.
  tpcds, tpch        benchmark schema generators only.

The demo_capi extension is not loaded but its sources are shipped
as a worked C API example under
/usr/share/doc/libduckdb-dev/examples/demo_capi/.

Publishing
----------

CI (.github/workflows/debian-package.yml) verifies that the source
package and the binaries build on Debian trixie, Debian sid, and
the supported Ubuntu LTS targets on every push to debian/latest.
Releases are published to OBS from the maintainer's laptop using
debian/scripts/publish.sh; CI does not have OBS credentials.  See
debian/README.OBS for one-time bootstrap and the publish loop.
